{
  "dom_id": "step-q124387",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14222207/assets/c5b92e2126df555db5f639bc9063586b6afc3bb2896b14edadedf183ec152ac5.png)\n\nThe point $P(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{1}{3})$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ?$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14222207/assets/df5d54940a136a53c1e1c00f8cc9d71d7b064f6e0895a029da2738cf21998a56.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ and $y=\\frac{1}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})}{(\\frac{1}{3})}=2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
